# Fair Game

Consider two-player games. 

One game might be "you buy a coin toss; heads you win the coin, tails you 
get nothing".

Another game might be: "if I discover a cure to cancer tomorrow, 
you win a million".

Another game might be: "I pay you $1000 in a year".

Another game might be: "you get a slice of the assets, 
cash flows and liabilities of a corporation".

You don't necessarily know the rules of the game, how long it takes, or how the
payoff works. Some of the gains might contain uncertainty, not just randomness.

But you see that people can freely play against each other, from either side of
the game, and you observe that a price emerges in each game. 

Some of these games can be priced reliably: the coin flip would be priced $0.5.
If it was priced $0.4, many people would want to toss, and nobody would want
to offer a coin. If it was $0.6, many people would offer their coins to be
tossed. Nobody would toss.

You can assume that they are fair games, meaning they are priced at their
expected value (Sum of probability * payoff, for all outcomes). After all,
if one side was advantaged, people who discern this advantage would rush to
take advantage of the mispricing, reaping the profits, while also correcting
the mispricing.

Since each game is priced at its expected value, i.e. its average outcome,
then it means the "upside" (the expected value of the outcomes where the
first player wins) equals the "downside" (the expected value of the outcomes
where the second player wins). If they weren't equal, they wouldn't average
out to the game's price; the price would move.

"Since today's price already bakes in the average future outcome, expected
upside must equal expected downside—otherwise the average would move."

Upside = downside.

Derive Put-Call parity.
